From: Michael R Sweet <msweet@msweet.org>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 15:06:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Validate IPP attribute names unconditionally
 (GHSA-w9hj-hq9p-m7f6)

Backport/squash of upstream OpenPrinting/cups master commits
f14d1fd8c64fe1e91718a4b910eca08c4087823c (2026-09-09 "Limit IPP
attribute names to IPP_MAX_KEYWORD"),
f7cd8e35d836d9842d6205319752b6832fce9851 (2026-09-18 "Restore name
buffer length check") and
f32341d7f9f9e1566e788dea329af4ac26da4fc7 (2026-09-16 "Validate IPP
attribute names unconditionally", GHSA-w9hj-hq9p-m7f6).

Attribute/member names were previously charset+length-checked only when
ippValidateAttribute() ran on a named attribute, while ipp_add_attr() and
the IPP reader accepted arbitrary name bytes (member names from
memberName carry no name through the reader path). With collection
attributes, a hostile printer response can steer member/attribute names
into PPD option names (e.g. job-presets-supported member names are
written as-is at ppd-cache.c:5165). This adds ipp_is_valid_keyword()
(RFC 8011 5.1.4 charset + <=255 length, matching upstream) and enforces
it in ipp_add_attr(), ippValidateAttribute() (names + keyword values) and
ipp_read_io() for both attribute names and collection memberNames, so
bad names are rejected at parse time.

Adaptation to 2.4.14 (master diverged: /* */ comment style, keyword
length cap, no bool helpers): helper is static int and returns 0/1; the
reader hunk targets ipp_read_io() name reads and the memberName case of
the collection parser. Keyword value length cap intentionally follows
master (255) inside the shared helper.

Upstream-commit: f14d1fd8c64fe1e91718a4b910eca08c4087823c
Upstream-commit: f7cd8e35d836d9842d6205319752b6832fce9851
Upstream-commit: f32341d7f9f9e1566e788dea329af4ac26da4fc7

